course: dictionary
lesson: 12065
title: Classy: stylish, polite or sarcastic
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Classy is an informal word. It means stylish, elegant and of good quality. «Her green dress was so classy» tells you the dress looked smart and expensive, not just pretty.
explain: Classy can also praise good behaviour. «He held the door open for everyone and said thank you.» That was a classy thing to do, because it was fair, polite and graceful. In sport, a classy player is very skilful.
explain: Watch out for the sarcastic kind. «Burping at the table — classy!» The speaker is not praising anyone. Read the clues: burping at the table is rude, so the word means the opposite of what it says. Swap in the opposite meaning and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Classy is an informal word, so you hear it in everyday talk more than in formal writing.
fact: «Classy» means stylish, elegant and of good quality, often looking expensive.
fact: «Classy» can also mean fair, polite and graceful, as in «a classy gesture».
fact: Said sarcastically, «classy» means the opposite: «Burping at the table — classy!» means it was rude.
fact: The comparison forms are classier and classiest: «That café is classier than the one next door.»
exercise: «The hotel lobby was classy, with gold lamps and soft carpets.» What does classy mean here? | Cold and empty | *Stylish, elegant and of good quality | Very loud and busy
exercise: «She let the younger players go first, which was a classy move.» What does classy suggest? | *Fair, polite and graceful behaviour | Slow and tired | Rude and selfish behaviour
exercise: Ben says, «Spilling juice on the sofa, classy!» What does he really mean? | *The opposite: it was rude and careless | He is praising the juice | It was very stylish and expensive
test: «The waiter folded the napkins into swans, which was a classy touch.» What does classy mean here? | Sad and tired | *Stylish and graceful | Messy and careless | Quick and noisy
test: Ella says, «Tripping over my own feet in the playground — classy!» Does she mean it? | Yes, it was very expensive | *No, she means it was embarrassing, the opposite of classy | Yes, she is praising how graceful she was
exam: What is the best meaning of classy in «Her classy black coat looked brand new and cost a lot.»? | Wet from the rain | *Stylish, elegant and expensive-looking | Loud and bright | Cheap and old
